Therapist: Hi Rachel, come on in. How has this past week been for you?
Client: It's been a hard one. Tuesday was the anniversary of my mom's death — three years now.
Therapist: Thank you for telling me that right away. How has this anniversary felt compared to the last two?
Client: Honestly, about the same amount of hard, which surprised me. I thought it would get easier each year, but Tuesday I could barely get out of bed.
Therapist: What did that day actually look like for you?
Client: I called in sick to the studio, which I never do. Just laid in bed most of the morning, then forced myself to at least shower by early afternoon.
Therapist: Did you reach out to anyone that day?
Client: My sister Ashley called around six, like she always does on that date. That helped some. We didn't talk about mom directly, just stayed on the phone while I made dinner.
Therapist: It sounds like Ashley has become a real anchor for you around this specific date each year.
Client: She really has. I don't know what I'd do without her checking in like that.
Therapist: How has your mood been on the other days this week, outside of Tuesday specifically?
Client: Some low days leading up to it, honestly, starting about Sunday. I could feel it coming, if that makes sense — like my body knew before I consciously thought about the date.
Therapist: That's a really common pattern with anniversary grief — the body often registers the date before the conscious mind catches up to it. Has your sleep or appetite shifted at all this week?
Client: Sleep's been rough, maybe five hours most nights since Sunday. Appetite's been okay, actually, better than some of my depressive stretches in the past.
Therapist: That's worth noting — some of your other symptom patterns held steady even while the mood dropped around the anniversary. I also want to ask about work, since I know you had that big client presentation coming up around this same time. How did that intersect with everything else this week?
Client: That's actually been its own mess. The Kestrel account pitch at the studio was due Wednesday, the day right after the anniversary, and I was so behind because of Tuesday that I ended up pulling most of Tuesday night to finish it.
Therapist: How did that go, both the work itself and how you felt doing it?
Client: The pitch actually went fine, my boss said the deck looked great. But I kept having this thought the whole time that I was a bad employee for needing the sick day in the first place, like I was being unprofessional by letting grief get in the way of work.
Therapist: That's a thought worth slowing down on, since it sounds like it added guilt on top of an already heavy day. What's the evidence that taking a single sick day on your mother's anniversary makes you a bad or unprofessional employee?
Client: When I actually say it out loud like that, not much. I've used maybe four sick days total in three years there.
Therapist: And is there anything that points the other direction — toward you actually being a reliable employee, even accounting for Tuesday?
Client: I mean, the Kestrel deck did turn out well, and my boss has never said anything critical about my work before this.
Therapist: So holding both of those pieces of evidence together, what's a fairer way to think about Tuesday, instead of "I was unprofessional"?
Client: Maybe, "I took one day I genuinely needed, and I still delivered good work right after." That feels a lot less harsh.
Therapist: That's a much more balanced and accurate read of what actually happened. I want to come back to the anniversary itself now, since that's clearly still carrying a lot of weight. Can you tell me more about what specifically came up for you on Tuesday? You don't have to go into everything, just whatever feels okay to share.
Client: I kept thinking about the last conversation we had, actually. She called me from the hospital and I was at work and told her I'd call back on my lunch break, and I never got the chance to.
Therapist: I notice you're tearing up right now, telling me that.
Client: Yeah. Sorry. It's still just so present, even three years later.
Therapist: There's nothing to apologize for — that's a profoundly painful memory to carry, and it makes complete sense that it surfaces with this kind of intensity, especially around the anniversary.
Client: I just keep thinking if I'd called her back sooner, maybe things would have gone differently.
Therapist: That thought — that calling back sooner might have changed the outcome — is that something you've sat with a lot over these three years?
Client: Almost constantly, if I'm honest. Especially in the first year.
Therapist: I want to check in with you right now, in this moment, since this is clearly bringing up a lot of activation for you. Would it be okay if we did a quick grounding exercise together, just to help settle your nervous system before we keep talking?
Client: Yeah, I think that would help. I can feel my heart racing a bit.
Therapist: Let's do the five-four-three-two-one technique. Can you name five things you can see in this room right now?
Client: The plant by the window, your bookshelf, the box of tissues, my water bottle, and the clock.
Therapist: Good. Now four things you can physically feel.
Client: The couch cushion under me, my feet on the floor, my sweater sleeve, and my hands in my lap.
Therapist: Now three things you can hear.
Client: The air conditioning, a car outside, and your voice.
Therapist: Two things you can smell.
Client: Um... the candle on your side table, and maybe just the room in general, like paper.
Therapist: And one thing you can taste.
Client: The coffee I had on the way here, still a little bit.
Therapist: How's your heart rate now compared to before we started?
Client: Actually a lot calmer. That always surprises me, how fast that works.
Therapist: It engages your senses in the present moment, which helps interrupt the loop your mind was in a minute ago. I want to gently go back to that thought you shared — the idea that calling your mom back sooner might have changed things. What would you say to a friend who told you they were carrying that exact same thought about someone they lost?
Client: I'd probably tell them it wasn't their fault, that they had no way of knowing, and that beating themselves up about it doesn't bring the person back, it just adds more pain on top of pain.
Therapist: That's a remarkably compassionate and clear response. What makes it harder to extend that same thinking to yourself?
Client: I don't know. It feels different when it's me. Like I should have known better somehow.
Therapist: That gap between how you'd treat a friend and how you treat yourself is something we've touched on before, and it sounds like it's still very present, especially around this specific memory. I'd like to try something new with you today, related to that memory specifically — would you be open to writing an unsent letter to your mom, as homework this week?
Client: What would that actually involve?
Therapist: You'd write her a letter you never intend to send — saying whatever you'd want her to know, including things about that last call if you want to go there. Some people find it helps move some of what's stuck in that memory into something more processed and complete-feeling.
Client: That feels intimidating, but also maybe exactly what I need. I've never actually written anything like that to her.
Therapist: There's no right way to do it, and you don't have to share the letter with me unless you want to — the value is mostly in the writing itself. Would you be willing to try a draft before our next session?
Client: Yeah, I think I can do that. I might need to work up to actually starting it, but I'll try.
Therapist: That's all I'm asking — an attempt, not a perfect finished letter. Sorry, before we go further, can I ask how things have been with Dr. Fitzgerald and the medication? I know she adjusted your sertraline dose last month.
Client: Sorry, one second — I'm getting a little choked up again, thinking about all of it at once. But yeah, she moved me up to 100 milligrams about five weeks ago.
Therapist: Take whatever time you need. And thank you for sharing that update — has the higher dose felt like it's made a difference, separate from the anniversary week specifically?
Client: I think so. My baseline mood on regular days felt a bit more stable the last month, even if this week was hard because of the date itself.
Therapist: That's an important distinction — the anniversary reaction can happen even when your underlying baseline is genuinely improving. Did Dr. Fitzgerald say anything about your diagnosis at that appointment, in terms of how she's thinking about where you're at?
Client: She said it's still major depressive disorder, recurrent, same as always, but that the recurrence pattern does seem tied pretty specifically to anniversary dates and certain triggers rather than being constant now, which she said is actually a good sign compared to a few years ago.
Therapist: That aligns with what I'm seeing too — you've built real capacity between episodes, even though the episodes themselves are still painful when they hit. I want to shift to something coming up that I know you've been anxious about — your cousin's wedding next month.
Client: Oh, yeah. I've been dreading bringing that up, honestly.
Therapist: What specifically feels hard about it?
Client: My mom's whole side of the family will be there, and I haven't seen most of them since the funeral. I keep thinking about just not going.
Therapist: What do you imagine would happen if you did go?
Client: Everyone asking how I'm doing, probably crying at some point in front of everyone, just feeling like the sad story of the family for the whole weekend.
Therapist: That sounds like a really specific, vivid fear. On a scale of how likely you think that actually is, from zero to a hundred, where would you put it?
Client: Maybe seventy. It feels pretty likely honestly.
Therapist: I'd like to propose something we haven't tried together yet — a small behavioral experiment. Instead of deciding now to avoid the whole weekend, what if you committed to just the rehearsal dinner on Friday night, treated it as a test, and then made the decision about Saturday's wedding itself afterward, based on how Friday actually went rather than how you're predicting it'll go right now?
Client: That actually feels more manageable than committing to the whole weekend upfront.
Therapist: Exactly the idea — it gives you real data instead of just the prediction. What would you specifically track on Friday, to compare against that seventy percent prediction?
Client: I guess whether people actually bring up mom directly, or whether it's more just normal wedding stuff. And whether I actually cry, or just feel emotional without it becoming a whole thing.
Therapist: Good, those are concrete and measurable. I'd like you to text yourself notes about that Friday night, and we'll go through what actually happened compared to the prediction next time, the same way we do with the thought records.
Client: Okay, I can do that. Ashley's actually going to be there too, so I could lean on her if it gets hard.
Therapist: That's a great built-in support to have in place already. Is there anything specific you'd want Ashley to do for you Friday night, if things do get hard, so you're not figuring that out in the moment?
Client: Probably just having her nearby, or a signal I could give her if I need to step outside for a minute.
Therapist: That's a smart, concrete plan to set up with her ahead of time rather than hoping it works itself out. Before we wrap up, I want to check a few administrative things, is that all right?
Client: Sure, go ahead.
Therapist: I want to confirm your contact info is current for our records — still the number ending in 0199?
Client: Yes, 555-0199 is still right, and the email is still rkowalski88@fakemail.example.
Therapist: Perfect, that matches what we have. And I saw your insurance renewed this year under the same Meridian Health Blue plan — has anything changed on that front, any new authorization needed?
Client: No, same plan, and my HR contact at Fenwick and Ito said the mental health benefit stayed the same for this year, so we should be fine continuing weekly.
Therapist: Good, that's one less thing to worry about. And just to confirm your address on file is still 88 Hollow Creek Road, unit four?
Client: Yes, still there.
Therapist: Great, everything's current. So, to summarize today — we processed some of what came up around the anniversary and the last phone call with your mom, reframed the guilt thought about taking a sick day around the Kestrel pitch, used the five senses grounding technique when things got activated, set up the unsent letter exercise as homework, talked through the medication update with Dr. Fitzgerald and where your diagnosis stands, and set up a behavioral experiment for Friday's rehearsal dinner to test your prediction about the wedding weekend, including a plan with Ashley for support if it gets hard.
Client: That's a lot, but it all feels connected somehow. I came in pretty shut down today and I don't feel that way anymore.
Therapist: That shift matters, and I want to name it — you did real, hard work today, especially revisiting that phone call memory and agreeing to try the letter. That's not easy material.
Client: Thank you. I think I needed to actually say some of that out loud instead of just carrying it alone.
Therapist: I'm glad you did. Let's plan on our regular time next week, and we'll start by hearing how Friday's dinner actually went, and how the letter felt to write.
Client: Sounds good. Thank you, really.
Therapist: Take good care of yourself, Rachel. I'll see you next week.
